 "notes": ["Q3 26: revenue and net income from Yahoo's earnings summary (the financial statements on Yahoo do not have these periods yet); other items are blank until they do.", "Cash flow from Yahoo Finance's cash-flow statement (Operating Cash Flow; capex = Capital Expenditure, shown as a positive number). FCF = operating cash flow - capex. FCF QoQ / YoY only between two positive values.", "Q4 25, FY22, FY23, FY24, FY25: net income from Yahoo's earnings summary, which matches diluted EPS x diluted shares (Yahoo's statement figure did not; income after tax is moved by the same amount).", "Q1 25: computed as the fiscal year minus its other three quarters where Yahoo's quarterly statements lack them; the fields are listed in \"derived\".", "Q3 25, Q3 26: diluted EPS from Yahoo's reported EPS where it matches net income / diluted shares, else net income / diluted shares (Yahoo's statements lack it).", "Fiscal labels are the company's own fiscal year, named by the calendar year it ends in.", "PER uses the trailing 4 quarters' diluted EPS (annual: the year's), ROA the trailing 4 quarters' net income over average total assets. EPS (GAAP, diluted) is the reported figure; EPS (adjusted) is Yahoo Finance's EPS on the analysts' basis (for US companies usually the company's adjusted, non-GAAP EPS; elsewhere usually the reported one), the basis of the (E) consensus. Each has its own QoQ / YoY, so a reported figure is never compared with an adjusted one.", "Yahoo lists the adjusted EPS of the last 4 quarters only: each daily run records what it shows (market/history/financials_eps_adjusted.jsonl), so older quarters stay. A fiscal year's adjusted EPS is Yahoo's annual figure, else the sum of its four quarters when all four are known (listed in \"derived\"), else blank."]
